{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cfpmxxp2DAZjfgqmabU8T","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. `src/str.js:7-12`, 7 tests passing.\n\n```js\nfunction slugify(s) {\n  return s\n    .toLowerCase()\n    .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-')\n    .replace(/^-+|-+$/g, '');\n}\n```\n\nNo separate trim step — whitespace is non-alphanumeric, so it collapses to `-` and the strip handles it, as the tests predicted.\n\n**The one assumption you haven't ruled on: `Café` → `caf`.** I shipped the literal spec reading, where \"alphanumeric\" means ASCII `[a-z0-9]`, so non-ASCII characters are silently dropped. I pinned it with a test (`test/str.test.js:31-37`) rather than leaving it undefined, specifically so a green suite isn't hiding an open decision — but the comment there marks it as unconfirmed, and this is the lossiest of the three options. If you have international input, this is wrong and you'll want one of:\n\n- keep the characters — `/[^\\p{L}\\p{N}]+/gu` → `café`\n- transliterate — `s.normalize('NFD').replace(/[\\u0300-\\u036f]/g,'')` before the existing regex → `cafe`\n\nBoth are one-line changes plus updating that test.\n\nOn verification, since it came up: six tests went red→green across the implementation, so their failure mode is proven. The non-ASCII test was written after the code and was green on first run, so I checked it separately against a Unicode-aware implementation — it returns `café`/`naïve-café` there versus `caf`/`na-ve-caf` shipped, so the test does discriminate rather than just agreeing with whatever I wrote.\n\nNot covered: non-string input throws on `.toLowerCase()`, matching `trim`'s existing behavior. I left that alone rather than inventing a convention the file doesn't have."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":572,"cache_read_input_tokens":33065,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tok